.TH OFI-PARSER-WORKER 8 "2026-11-01" "OpenForensic Imager 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ofi-parser-worker \- isolated parser worker for OpenForensic Imager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ofi-parser-worker
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ofi-parser-worker
executes the operations that touch evidence. It reads one JSON job request
from standard input, performs the job, and writes one JSON response to standard
output. It is not meant to be run by hand; the application starts one process
per job and reads the response.
.PP
Each process serves exactly one request. Before the job runs, the worker
applies, in order: resource limits, a Landlock path allowlist built from the
request's own paths, and a default-deny seccomp syscall filter. It also sets
.B PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
before either filter, so nothing it does can gain privileges it did not hold.
.PP
The worker refuses the three operations that do not parse evidence content
\(em \fBevidence_list\fR, \fBauditAppend\fR, and \fBauditVerify\fR \(em with the
error code
.BR worker.unsupported_operation .
Those belong to the trusted process. The refusal fails closed in both
directions: the trusted process will not parse evidence, and the worker will
not touch the case database.
.PP
The process is single-request by design, so a parser defect cannot persist
across jobs: the application cancels a job by killing the worker's process
group, and the next job starts from a fresh process.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The request was read and a response was written. A response can still carry a
job error; the exit status describes the worker, not the job.
.TP
.B 1
The worker could not write a response, for example if no request arrived on
standard input.
.SH SECURITY
The worker is confined, but it is not a privilege boundary. It runs as the
invoking user, so a kernel or sandbox escape, or a defect in a native parser
that yields arbitrary code execution, compromises that user's authority rather
than a lesser one. See
.I /usr/share/doc/openforensic-imager/SECURITY_MODEL.md
for the boundaries and the residual risks that remain accepted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openforensic-imager (8)
